	'pg_about_t' => 'About Himuro Gelato',
	'pg_about_lede' => 'Himuro Gelato was founded in 2019 by Ines Varga in a former cold-store on Quay Lane. We churn four litres at a time so every scoop stays close to the paddle.',
	'pg_about_h1' => 'From cold-store to cold slab',
	'pg_about_p1' => 'Founded in 2019 by Ines Varga, Himuro Gelato occupies a former dockside cold-store at 14 Quay Lane in the Harbour Quarter. Ines opened the workshop with a simple aim: to make small-pan gelato scaled entirely to the pace of Larkhaven evenings. Rather than filling industrial display cases that sit under bright lights for days, the kitchen works with small, dense batches that turn over quickly. The high ceiling and thick walls of the original cold-store keep the space calm and cold, providing an ideal setting for careful balancing, seasonal fruit preparation, and quiet morning churns. Every tub and cup handed across the glass counter reflects this steady craft.',
	'pg_about_h2' => 'Why we churn in small batches',
	'pg_about_p2' => 'We churn four litres at a time because gelato begins to lose its intended texture the moment it leaves the batch freezer. Large containers invite temperature swings and ice crystallization as scoops are repeatedly drawn. By limiting each freeze to four litres and eleven minutes, the ice crystals stay microscopic and the natural milk sugars coat the tongue evenly. This modest scale also means our kitchen can adjust recipes day by day. When fruit arrives riper or the harbour air cools, Amara Okafor can alter fruit ratios, and Tomas Lind can calibrate morning churns so that no batch lingers.',
	'pg_about_h3' => 'Three house rules for every batch',
	'pg_about_p3' => 'Three house rules govern our daily work at 14 Quay Lane. Rule one: the base always rests overnight for twelve hours at 4 °C so milk proteins and fruit aromas fully integrate before freezing. Rule two: we churn only four litres per batch in an eleven-minute cycle, never stretching time or volume for convenience. Rule three: gelato must harden for two hours at −22 °C, then be tempered and served strictly at −12 °C. What is scooped today was churned today, ensuring that the cold sweetness you carry home is fresh and dense.',
